    {'para': 'In the D., Ab.-Gen., and L. sing., and G. pl., these stems sometimes follow ⟨nadī⟩; thus, ⟨dhiyai⟩, ⟨dhiyās⟩, ⟨dhiyām⟩, ⟨dhīnām⟩. Cf. § 185. Observe that where the case-ending begins with a vowel the stem-final ⟨ī⟩ is split into ⟨iy⟩.'},
    {'para': '**190.** The following additional prefixes are used with verbs: ⟨adhi⟩ ‘over, above, on’; ⟨api⟩ ‘unto, close upon’∗; ⟨abhi⟩ ‘to, unto’, ‘against’ (often with implied violence); ⟨ni⟩ ‘down, into, in’; ⟨prati⟩ ‘back to, against, in return’; ⟨vi⟩ ‘apart, away, out’.'},
    {'para': '**191.** Both in verbal forms and in derivatives, the final ⟨i⟩ or ⟨u⟩ of a prefix ordinarily lingualizes the initial ⟨s⟩ of a root to which it is prefixed; and, in a few cases, the ⟨ṣ⟩ remains even after an interposed ⟨a⟩ of augment or reduplication; thus, from ⟨sad⟩ + ⟨ni⟩, ⟨niṣīdati⟩; ⟨sthā⟩ + ⟨adhi⟩, pres. pass. ⟨adhiṣṭhīyate⟩, impf. pass. ⟨adhyaṣṭhīyata⟩.'},
    {'para': '**192.** The final ⟨s⟩ of prefixes in ⟨is⟩ and ⟨us⟩ becomes ⟨ṣ⟩ before initial ⟨k⟩, ⟨kh⟩, ⟨p⟩, ⟨ph⟩; thus, from ⟨pad⟩ + ⟨nis⟩, ⟨niṣpadyate⟩.'},
